[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) was merged as one issue: the parser reads dates month first. The
reports in it describe three problems. Each now has a ticket of its own, with its count.

<Callout kind="warn">
The queued parser fix closes only the first row. The booking-v3 hint and the one-day shift
stay broken after it ships.
</Callout>

## The split

<ProblemSplit
  ticket={4471}
  issues={{
    parser: 'https://github.com/r2luna/support-kb/issues/1',
    hint: 'https://github.com/r2luna/support-kb/issues/2',
    'one-day': 'https://github.com/r2luna/support-kb/issues/3',
  }}
/>

- **`parser`**: the shared parser reads month first, and every tenant here writes the day first.
  Day and month swap. Reports come from every channel except the booking-v3 form.
- **`hint`**: booking-v3 prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field. People type what it says, and
  the form reads the date day first. These reports come only through the form, at tenants on
  booking-v3. This is a different bug, with a different fix.
- **`one-day`**: the date moves by one day, not by a swap. Different zones see different days,
  and some months only (the desk's `oneDayMove` notes). This looks like a time zone or DST bug.
  #4471's cause table footnoted these reports under the parser.

## How the reports were counted

1. The export pages overlap, so rows were de-duplicated by report id.
2. Each report was placed by its tenant's locale and booking form and by its channel
   (`causes.ts`), not by intake's tag. The tag is a guess from the browser, and the
   "intake tag differs" column shows how often that guess is wrong.
3. Reports in the desk's `oneDayMove` notes were taken out as their own problem.

## Since April

<QueueTimeline ticket={4471} />

## Lessons

- A subject line says how a report feels, not what broke. Read the body, the tenant, and the way in.
- A footnote under the wrong cause hides a problem. If it doesn't fit the cause, it gets a ticket.

